WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.480
In an asymmetric division, a radial glial cell keeps one daughter.

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.680
What happens to the other one?

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.160
It can take one of two routes.

4
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:18.200
In direct neurogenesis, the radial glial cell divides into another radial glial cell

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.200
and a neuron.

6
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:24.713
In indirect neurogenesis, the second daughter is an intermediate progenitor.

7
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:29.467
It lets go of the ventricle and moves into the subventricular zone.

8
00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:34.247
There it divides once more, in rodent cortex usually into two neurons.

9
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.000
Filming rat slices, Noctor and colleagues tracked both routes.

10
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.380
Most radial glial divisions were asymmetric.

11
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:47.643
About nine in ten intermediate progenitor divisions gave two neurons.

12
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:52.803
But only 19 divisions away from the ventricle were seen, so read it as rat.

13
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:58.377
In mouse, intermediate progenitors may make most cortical projection neurons.

14
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:03.927
Transcription factors, proteins that switch genes on or off, mark the steps.

15
00:01:04.907 --> 00:01:06.347
Pax6 marks radial glia.

16
00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:09.747
Tbr2 marks intermediate progenitors.

17
00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:12.247
Tbr1 marks young neurons.

18
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.900
They appear in that order, as cells move outward.

19
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:18.703
But are the markers only labels?

20
00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:24.283
In 2008, two groups removed Tbr2 from the developing mouse brain.

21
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:28.383
Count mitoses at the ventricle, and away from it.

22
00:01:28.923 --> 00:01:32.743
If Tbr2 is a step in the indirect route, which count should fall?

23
00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:41.217
Division at the ventricle went on.

24
00:01:41.997 --> 00:01:43.157
Division away from it fell.

25
00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:45.817
And fewer cortical neurons were made.

26
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:51.587
The position of mitosis, read with the markers, pointed to the lost progenitor.

27
00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:55.907
In those mice, Tbr2 was needed for the indirect route.

28
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.160
Primates have a third progenitor in far greater numbers.

29
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.980
Outer radial glia divide far from the ventricle.

30
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.540
Mouse has a few. Humans have far more.

31
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:10.723
Now, what does a leaving daughter give up?

32
00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:14.923
A niche is the local setting that keeps a stem cell a stem cell.

33
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:19.007
The apical foot faces the cerebrospinal fluid.

34
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:24.887
In mouse, that fluid carries growth factors, among them IGF2, that support

35
00:02:24.887 --> 00:02:25.887
proliferation.

36
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.110
The basal foot rests on the basement membrane under the pia.

37
00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.690
Neighbouring cells, through Notch, are part of the setting too.

38
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.440
In an asymmetric division, the daughter that leaves lets go of the apical surface.

39
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.360
It gives up the fluid side of the niche, and its growth signals.

40
00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:46.063
The daughter that stays keeps it.

41
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:51.133
Losing apical contact goes with leaving, but it is not the same thing.

42
00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:55.613
An intermediate progenitor has let go, and still divides once more.

43
00:02:56.517 --> 00:03:00.957
So a spent daughter makes one neuron directly, or usually two through an

44
00:03:00.957 --> 00:03:02.137
intermediate progenitor.

45
00:03:02.997 --> 00:03:05.337
Follow its markers and divisions to tell which.
